catalogue-license lppl1.3
catalogue-topics japanese multilingual-addon

name babel-kazakh
shortdesc Kazakh language support for pdf/Xe/LuaLaTeX with babel and polyglossia
longdesc We recently (February, 2026) developed a hyphenation dictionary
longdesc for the Kazakh language. After completing this project, the
longdesc natural question arose of developing official support for the
longdesc Kazakh language in TeX. Kazakh language support has been
longdesc implemented to work with XeLaTeX, LuaLaTeX, and pdfLaTeX macro
longdesc packages, they all work with babel package. TeX Live 2025 on
longdesc Linux was used for testing. Hyphenation patterns support files
longdesc were sent to maintainers. XeLaTeX and LuaLaTeX support is also
longdesc compatible with polyglossia. We provide also sample files, to
longdesc show the work of our package. BibTeX database file is also
longdesc included to demonstrate compatibility with BibLaTeX. Notice
longdesc that BibLaTeX support is not complete yet in this initial
longdesc version, 1.0, it will be completed soon.
catalogue-ctan /macros/latex/required/babel/contrib/babel-kazakh
catalogue-license lppl
catalogue-topics french multilingual-addon
catalogue-version 1.0

name babel-kurmanji
shortdesc Babel support for Kurmanji
longdesc The package provides the language definition file for support
longdesc of Kurmanji in babel. Kurmanji belongs to the family of Kurdish
longdesc languages. Some shortcuts are defined, as well as translations
longdesc to Kurmanji of standard LaTeX names. Note that the package is
longdesc dealing with Northern Kurdish, written using a Latin-based
longdesc alphabet. The arabxetex package offers support for Kurdish
longdesc written in Arabic script.
catalogue-ctan /macros/latex/contrib/babel-contrib/kurmanji
catalogue-license lppl1.3
catalogue-topics kurdish kurmanji multilingual-addon
catalogue-version 1.1

name babel-latin
shortdesc Babel support for Latin
longdesc The babel-latin package provides the babel languages latin,
longdesc classicallatin, medievallatin, and ecclesiasticallatin. It also
longdesc defines several useful shorthands as well as some modifiers for
longdesc typographical fine-tuning.
