name ltb2bib
shortdesc Converts amsrefs' .ltb bibliographical databases to BibTeX format
longdesc This package implements a LaTeX command that converts an
longdesc amsrefs bibliographical database (.ltb) to a BibTeX
longdesc bibliographical database (.bib). ltb2bib is the reverse of the
longdesc amsxport option in amsrefs. Typical uses are: produce bib
longdesc entries for some publishers which don't accept amsrefs (Taylor
longdesc & Francis, for example); import an ltb database in a database
longdesc management program, e.g. for sorting; access one's ltb database
longdesc within emacs's RefTeX mode.
catalogue-also amsrefs
catalogue-ctan /biblio/bibtex/utils/ltb2bib
catalogue-license lppl1.3
catalogue-topics biblio
catalogue-version 0.01

name ltmermaid
shortdesc Embed Mermaid diagrams in LaTeX
longdesc Embed Mermaid diagrams in LaTeX. Diagram sources are written to
longdesc disk, and the Mermaid CLI (mmdc) renders vector PDFs, which are
longdesc then included using \includegraphics. mermaid.sty is the
longdesc implementation to be used with
longdesc pdfLaTeX/XeLaTeX/upLaTeX/LuaLaTeX, etc. It invokes the Mermaid
longdesc CLI via the shellesc package (or directly via \write18). No Lua
longdesc interpreter is required. ltmermaid.sty is an implementation
longdesc dedicated to LuaLaTeX that provides the same macros (the
longdesc Renderer option, \Mermaid... macros, and the mermaid
longdesc environment) as mermaid.sty. The CLI is invoked via Lua's
longdesc os.execute.
catalogue-contact-repository https://github.com/ryoya9826/ltMermaid
catalogue-ctan /macros/latex/contrib/ltmermaid
catalogue-license lppl1.3c
catalogue-topics diagram
catalogue-version 1.0c

name ltx-talk
shortdesc A class for typesetting (accessible) presentations
longdesc This class is experimental, and changes may occur to
longdesc interfaces. Development is focussed on tagging/functionality as
longdesc the primary driver; as such, support for design aspects is
longdesc likely to be lower priority. It requires LaTeX 2025-11-01 or
longdesc later. The ltx-talk class is focused on producing (on-screen)
longdesc presentations, along with support material such as handouts and
longdesc speaker notes. Content is created in a frame environment, each
longdesc of which can be divided up into a number of slides (actual
longdesc output pages). A simple overlay notation is used to specify
longdesc which material appears on each slide within a frame. The class
longdesc supports a range of environments to enable complex slide
longdesc relationships to be constructed. The appearance of slides is
longdesc controlled by a template system. Many of the elements of slides
longdesc can be adjusted by setting simple key-based values in the
longdesc preamble. More complex changes can be implemented by altering
longdesc specific, targeted definitions without needing to rewrite
longdesc entire blocks of code. This allows a variety of visual
longdesc appearances to be selected for the same content source. The
longdesc ltx-talk class has syntax similar to the popular beamer class,
longdesc although there are some (deliberate) differences. However,
longdesc ltx-talk has been implemented to support creation of tagged
longdesc (accessible) PDF output as a core aim. As such, it is suited to
longdesc creating output for reuse in other formats, e.g. HTML
longdesc conversions, without additional steps.
catalogue-also beamer
catalogue-contact-bugs https://github.com/josephwright/ltx-talk/issues
catalogue-contact-home https://www.texdev.net/ltx-talk
catalogue-contact-repository https://github.com/josephwright/ltx-talk
catalogue-ctan /macros/latex/contrib/ltx-talk
catalogue-license lppl1.3c
catalogue-topics class presentation tagged-pdf
catalogue-version 0.6.10

